name: Mastodon work session - audit the account-record contract before a rework
repo: mastodon
contract_symbol: Account
contract_file: app/models/account.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 4 - a RE-QUESTION on a live anchor.
# It exists so the next phase can run the mini-bench - both arms, once, at the real wall,
# unscored - against a real baseline. Nothing here is a verdict: no delta is claimed and
# none has been measured on this shape.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION, AND THE NUMBER THAT KILLED IT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Previous yaml kept at `mastodon.yaml.table-altitude.bak` (cycle 3). Cycle 2's is at
# `mastodon.yaml.line-not-file.bak`, cycle 1's at `mastodon.yaml.class-call-sites.bak`.
#
# Cycle 3's description opened "about to rework how a user account record is STORED and
# looked up - the record that both local users and remote actors are ROWS IN", and its axis
# said each dependent reaches the record "in whatever form the surrounding code already
# used".
#
#   MEASURED (results/loop/mastodon/minibench.md, both arms x1, exit 0, no watchdog,
#   274s and 216s of a 480s wall, both `valid: true`, citation grounding 1.0 on both arms):
#     baseline `dependents`  2 of 22 = 0.091   (gate: at or below 0.50)  PASSES
#     sense    `dependents`  2 of 22 = 0.091
#     delta                          = 0.000   (gate: at least +0.50)    FAILS
#
# WHAT WENT WRONG WAS THE ALTITUDE OF THE WORD "RECORD", and it was in the description, not
# in the axis. `stored` + `rows in` read as *the database row*, and BOTH arms audited the
# TABLE rather than the CLASS. The baseline's 22 tool calls were 18 searches, every one of
# them aimed at the SQL string for the table - `create_table`, the foreign keys, the Scenic
# views, `arel_table`, `find_by_sql`, the streaming SQL - two of them pruning by hand with
# `grep -vE`. The sense arm ran the SAME route: three MCP calls in step 1, then step 2 was
# nine `Bash` greps and no MCP call at all; `sense_blast` was never invoked. Both arms
# produced ~70 grounded, jumpable citations. They were the wrong ~70, and they were the SAME
# wrong ~70, which is why `sense-only` and `baseline-only` were both ZERO.
#
# The judge, independently, on both arms (baseline step 2 `map_quality` 0.35, sense step 2
# 0.20): "audits the accounts TABLE (raw SQL, schema, views, streaming SQL) but almost
# entirely misses the Ruby-code dependents the rubric enumerates ... It picked one altitude
# (SQL/schema) and stopped."
#
# Arithmetic check, run: a baseline at 0.091 caps the achievable delta at 1.00 - 0.091 =
# 0.909, so +0.50 was reachable. The ceiling did not miss the bar; the arms did.
#
# WHAT CHANGED THIS CYCLE (anchor kept, per laws.md ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR):
#   1. THE STORAGE READING IS CLOSED OFF EXPLICITLY. The rework is now framed as a change to
#      what the CLASS offers other code - the class-level methods it answers, the query
#      scopes chained off it, the constants declared on it, the objects it hands back - and
#      the description says in as many words that how it is persisted is NOT changing. Step 2
#      then rules the storage layer out of the answer: a line that reaches the underlying
#      table, its columns, a key relating one to another, or a hand-written query naming any
#      of those is declared NOT a dependency on the class and NOT what is wanted. This is a
#      NEGATIVE boundary - it names what does not count, never where the answers live, so the
#      inventory prohibition still binds.
#   2. THE MECHANISM SENTENCE IS NOW IN SOURCE TERMS. Cycle 3 said dependents reach the
#      record "in whatever form the surrounding code already used", and both arms read that
#      as licence to chase the lowercase table token. The axis now says HOW the dependency is
#      taken in source: the dependent NAMES THE CLASS ITSELF and then calls a class-level
#      method on it, chains one of its query scopes, reads a constant declared on it, tests
#      an object's type against it, or passes the class along as a value to something
#      generic. No shared method, helper or gateway - that property is unchanged and true.
#   3. THE TWO ROWS THE CYCLE-3 BASELINE CITED MOVE TO THE NON-SCORING ANCHOR GROUP.
#      `d:friends-of-friends` and `d:suggestions-source` were the only two `dependents` rows
#      EITHER arm took, and both arms took both - that is the definition of an anchor. They
#      are the two rows the table route happens to walk into (one runs raw SQL through the
#      class, the other is a scope chain in the same directory). Re-gold from what the
#      baseline MISSED, per the RE-QUESTION rule.
#   4. FOUR NEW ROWS ADMITTED from the shown blast set, chosen for being class-surface and
#      unreachable from a table grep: a scope read in an audit-log controller, a scope read
#      in a search-index importer, a constant read in a text-extraction helper, and a scope
#      merged into a relationships endpoint. Each was opened and its line read by hand.
#      `Account.representative` rows (`Admin::ModerationAction#mark_statuses_as_sensitive!`,
#      `Admin::SystemCheck::MediaPrivacyCheck#create_test_attachment!`) stay OUT, unchanged
#      from cycle 3: that token was already earned by a cycle-2 baseline grep. An authoring
#      preference, not a screen - no draft was killed by a grep here.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Account` (app/models/account.rb) is the record every other model, service, worker and
# controller in Mastodon hangs off: local users and remote actors are both rows in it. It is
# the thing a maintainer reworking identity would actually open first. Anchor UNCHANGED from
# cycle 1 - the loop does not re-scout a contract that has been measured, and cycle 3 proved
# the anchor carries (below). This is NOT a NO-ANCHOR.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, `Account --file app/models/account.rb`, RE-RUN this
# cycle at the current checkout, unchanged from cycles 2 and 3):
#   app caller files : 107   (total incl test/non-app: 107)
#   SCATTER (dirs)   : 34
#   GREP-NOISE       : 'Account' in 257 app files   PRECISION=0.42
#   VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
# The three candidates profiled in cycle 2 are kept on the record so a later cycle does not
# repeat them; none beat 0.42:
#   Account                 0.42  (107 callers / 257 grep files, 34 dirs)  <- kept
#   ResolveAccountService   0.94  (16 callers / 17 grep files, 9 dirs) - near-transcription,
#     the profile laws.md records as handing a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. REJECTED.
#   AccountReachFinder      1.17  (7 callers / 6 grep files, 3 dirs) - above 1.00 means false
#     caller edges, and 7 files cannot yield twelve rows anyway. REJECTED.
#   AccountStat             RESOLVER-GAP (0 app caller files).
# Two facet hunts, also kept on the record as dead ends:
#   - `Account::Interactions`: `sense_blast` returns ZERO direct callers; its 20 indirect
#     callers are all "via Account", i.e. the same caller set one hop out.
#   - Instance-method dispatch does not resolve across files here. `sense_graph
#     Account#local? --direction callers` returns 12 callers, ALL inside account.rb; the
#     seventeen `Account::Interactions` predicates return 3 app caller files between them
#     across 2 directories, every other caller a spec. There is no method-level facet.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS (the whole thesis, and what the mini-bench measures)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dependents of this contract share NO entry point. There is no method, no helper, no
# gateway that they pass through: each one NAMES THE CLASS ITSELF in source and then does
# one of five things with it - calls a class-level method on it, chains one of its query
# scopes, reads a constant declared on it, tests an object's type against it, or hands the
# class along as a value to something generic. So enumerating the callers of any one PART of
# the contract cannot reach them; only the class as a whole has them all. And because most
# of these files are not about the record's own concerns, its name recurs in them for
# reasons that have nothing to do with the dependency, so finding the file is not finding
# the dependency - one line is.
#
# MEASURED SUPPORT FOR THE MECHANISM, on this gold set. Of the 24 `dependents` rows, ZERO
# call any of the five finder methods the cycle-2 sense arm walked (`find_local`,
# `find_remote`, `representative`, `find_local!`, `find_remote!`) - the rows that DID sit
# near that surface are exactly the ones in the anchor group. The 24 lines reach the class
# through 17 distinct expressions: `.find`, `.exists?`, `.where`, `.sum`, `.local`,
# `.inboxes`, `.auditable`, `.searchable`, `.model_name`, `.find_or_initialize_by`,
# `.without_suspended`, `.without_internal`, `is_a?`, `when`, a bare-class argument, and two
# class constants (`Account::MENTION_RE`, `Account::URL_PREFIX_RE`). No method-caller walk
# enumerates that, and no single expression covers more than a handful of rows.
#
# NOT ONE GOLD ROW IS A STORAGE DEPENDENCY. All 24 are source lines that name the class.
# That is what makes the negative boundary in step 2 honest rather than a trick: an arm that
# audits the table comes back visibly empty instead of comfortably full, which is exactly
# what cycle 3 could not make happen.
#
# WHAT A GREP PRINTS, RECORDED AND NEVER A GATE (laws.md: NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY
# FORM). `Account` appears in 257 app files against 107 real caller files - precision 0.42,
# the lowest profiled on this repo. Cycle 3's baseline ran eighteen distinct searches and
# reached 2 of 22; the negative-filter shape of several of them (`| grep -vE`) is the tell
# that it was drowning in occurrences and pruning by hand. This is RECORDED; it decides
# nothing, and the two-arm run in the next phase is what decides.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN. `sense_blast Account` is AMBIGUOUS at MCP defaults (13 symbols named
# Account; the tool returns a hint naming the `file` parameter plus top matches, so an arm
# pays one extra call to disambiguate). `sense_blast Account --file app/models/account.rb`,
# re-probed this cycle via bench/lib/mcp_probe.py at MCP defaults with no `min_confidence`,
# returns 200 direct callers over 30 areas, risk high, truncated. EVERY one of the 24
# `dependents` rows below appears in that shown payload - checked row by row against the
# probe output this cycle, all OK, zero MISS - and each row's line sits INSIDE the enclosing
# `def` the payload names, because the payload gives the enclosing `def` and not the line
# that touches the contract. Each line below was opened and read by hand.
#
# MEMORIZATION (memorization_probe.py, per candidate row). CUT as recited and staying cut:
# `FeedManager` (0.727), `InstancePresenter` (0.842). The four rows admitted this cycle were
# all probed: `Importer::AccountsIndexImporter`, `Admin::ActionLogsController` and
# `Api::V1::Accounts::RelationshipsController` all come back `recall: null` (too few declared
# members to score recitation). `Extractor` came back at recall 0.6, and the probe itself
# labels it "clears 0.3 but only 3 member(s) named (< 5) - generic framework vocabulary, not
# recitation of THIS symbol"; the three names it recited are its own public extract-* methods,
# and this row's credit is a line-level fact (`text.scan(Account::MENTION_RE)` at :28), not a
# member list, so it is admitted on the same reasoning `ApplicationController` (0.30) was in
# cycle 3. The anchor itself comes back recited (`Account` 0.312) - that is the contract's own
# API surface, which is why the contract centre is a NON-SCORING anchor group; no gold row's
# credit is a member list.
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework a class - the one whose objects both local users
  and remote actors are. What is changing is what that class offers the rest of the
  codebase: the class-level methods it answers, the query scopes chained off it, the
  constants declared on it, and the objects it hands back. How it is persisted is NOT
  changing and the storage layer is not in scope. Before you touch it, audit the code
  that depends on that class, completely enough that the rework cannot silently break a
  dependent. What counts is the exact line at which a dependency is taken, not the file
  it sits in: for each one give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to,
  plus one phrase on how it uses the class. A filename with no line number does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Identify the class under change
  prompt: "Task: orient. In a few sentences plus exact file:line references, name the
    single class this ticket is about, say where it is defined, and say what a rework of
    what it offers other code would have to preserve. Settle on the class as a whole - the
    audit that follows is about everything in the codebase that depends on that class, not
    about any one part of it and not about how it is stored. Give file:line for everything
    you name.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the class under change
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket. Find EVERY place in the codebase that
    depends on the class you just identified, so the rework cannot silently break one. Be
    exhaustive and group them by area. These dependents share no entry point: there is no
    method, helper or gateway they all pass through, so enumerating what calls any one part
    of the class will not find them. What each one does instead is name the class itself in
    source and then do something with it - call a class-level method on it, chain one of its
    query scopes, read a constant declared on it, test an object's type against it, or hand
    the class along as a value to something generic. There is no single shape to look for.
    A line that reaches the persisted form instead - the underlying table, its columns, a key
    relating one to another, or a hand-written query naming any of those - is not a
    dependency on the class and is NOT what I need here; if a line does not name the class
    itself in source, it does not count. Most of the code that does depend on it is not about
    that class's own concerns at all: it consults the class to settle a question about
    something else entirely. In those files the class's name also recurs many times over for
    reasons that have nothing to do with the dependency, so identifying the file is not the
    same as identifying the dependency: one line in it is the dependency, and that line is
    what I need. For each dependent give file:line and one phrase on how it uses the class.
    A filename with no line number does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression
    shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Pinned the dependency line in each file, not just the file
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the account class. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (9, NON-SCORING anchor): the class itself, the obvious centre both arms
#     reach, the three rows the cycle-2 baseline cited, and the two rows BOTH arms cited in
#     cycle 3. It does not discriminate and it is not meant to.
#   - `dependents` (24, THE discriminator): ONE row per FILE, across 19 directories. Every
#     row is in the shown `sense_blast` output above, every row's line sits inside the
#     enclosing `def` that payload names, and every credit below was read off its own line
#     by hand (gold_audit stamp/verify), never taken from a script tally.
gold:
  # --- NON-SCORING anchors: the contract centre, plus every row a measured arm has reached ---
  - {id: contract-model, group: contract, match: [models/account.rb], relation: "app/models/account.rb:65 `class Account < ApplicationRecord` - the account class itself, the contract under change"}
  - {id: c:status, group: contract, match: [models/status.rb], relation: "app/models/status.rb:77 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :statuses` - the content model hangs off the account association"}
  - {id: c:user, group: contract, match: [models/user.rb], relation: "app/models/user.rb:72 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :user` - a local login is a User row pointing at an Account row"}
  - {id: c:resolve-service, group: contract, match: [services/resolve_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/resolve_account_service.rb:27 `@account ||= Account.find_remote(@username, @domain)` - the webfinger resolve path, the obvious lookup entry point for accounts"}
  - {id: c:account-owned-concern, group: contract, match: [account_owned_concern.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/account_owned_concern.rb:21 `@account = username_param.present? ? Account.find_local!(username_param) : Account.local.find(account_id_param)` - the shared account-owned page concern resolves its subject through two class finders; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 2"}
  - {id: c:json-ld-helper, group: contract, match: [helpers/json_ld_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/json_ld_helper.rb:233 `on_behalf_of ||= Account.representative` - unsigned fetches fall back to the instance representative account; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 2"}
  - {id: c:tag-manager-local-lookup, group: contract, match: [activitypub/tag_manager.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/tag_manager.rb:260 `Account.local.with_username(usernames).or(Account.local.where(id: ids))` - URI-to-actor translation resolves local actor URIs through the class scope; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 2"}
  - {id: c:friends-of-friends, group: contract, match: [friends_of_friends_source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/friends_of_friends_source.rb:10 `Account.find_by_sql([<<~SQL.squish, { id: account.id, limit: limit }])` - the friends-of-friends suggestion runs raw SQL through the class; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 3, reached from the table route"}
  - {id: c:suggestions-source, group: contract, match: [account_suggestions/source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/source.rb:13 `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.searchable.where(discoverable: true).without_silenced.without_memorial` - the follow-suggestion base scope; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 3, reached from the table route"}
  # --- THE DISCRIMINATOR: one dependency line per file, in a file whose subject is elsewhere ---
  - {id: d:activity-uri-resource, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/activity.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity.rb:75 `ActivityPub::TagManager.instance.uri_to_resource(uri, Account)` - inbound activity handling hands the class itself to a generic URI resolver as the type to resolve into"}
  - {id: d:activity-create-delivered-to, group: dependents, match: [activity/create.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity/create.rb:170 `@delivered_to_account ||= Account.find(@options[:delivered_to_account_id])` - status creation memoises the delivery target by id to decide local visibility"}
  - {id: d:favourited-by-accounts, group: dependents, match: [statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb:22 `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.without_suspended.includes(:favourites, :account_stat, :user)` - the favourited-by list builds its preload scope off the bare class"}
  - {id: d:reblogged-by-accounts, group: dependents, match: [statuses/reblogged_by_accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses/reblogged_by_accounts_controller.rb:22 `Account.without_suspended.includes(:statuses, :account_stat, :user).references(:statuses)` - the reblogged-by list builds its preload scope off the class"}
  - {id: d:authorize-interactions-dispatch, group: dependents, match: [authorize_interactions_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/authorize_interactions_controller.rb:11 `when Account` - the remote-follow landing page branches on the resolved resource being an account rather than a status"}
  - {id: d:signature-verification, group: dependents, match: [concerns/signature_verification.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/signature_verification.rb:48 `signed_request_actor.is_a?(Account) ? signed_request_actor : nil` - HTTP signature verification type-tests the signing actor against the class before treating it as an account"}
  - {id: d:inbox-unknown-actor, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/inboxes_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/inboxes_controller.rb:30 `... && !Account.exists?(uri: json['actor'])` - the last clause of a five-clause boolean drops Delete/Update activities for actors the instance has never stored"}
  - {id: d:followers-sync-uri-prefix, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/followers_synchronizations_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/followers_synchronizations_controller.rb:20 `signed_request_account.uri[Account::URL_PREFIX_RE]` - follower-list synchronisation slices a partner's URI with a pattern constant declared on the class"}
  - {id: d:admin-api-set-account, group: dependents, match: [v1/admin/accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/admin/accounts_controller.rb:103 `@account = Account.find(params[:id])` - the admin API resolves its subject by primary key; the file's other thirteen mentions of the name are serializers, services, workers and filters"}
  - {id: d:admin-base-set-user, group: dependents, match: [admin/base_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/base_controller.rb:21 `@user = Account.find(params[:account_id]).user || raise(ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound)` - the admin base controller reaches a login by going through the account record first"}
  - {id: d:action-logs-auditable, group: dependents, match: [admin/action_logs_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/action_logs_controller.rb:9 `@auditable_accounts = Account.auditable.select(:id, :username).order(username: :asc)` - the audit-log index builds its moderator filter list from a named class scope"}
  - {id: d:relationships-without-suspended, group: dependents, match: [accounts/relationships_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/accounts/relationships_controller.rb:9 `@accounts.merge!(Account.without_suspended) unless truthy_param?(:with_suspended)` - the relationships endpoint conditionally merges a class scope into an already-built relation"}
  - {id: d:single-user-mode, group: dependents, match: [controllers/application_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/application_controller.rb:108 `@single_user_mode ||= Rails.configuration.x.single_user_mode && Account.without_internal.exists?` - the base controller decides whether the whole instance runs in single-user mode through a class scope predicate"}
  - {id: d:serializer-decorator-model-name, group: dependents, match: [rest/account_serializer.rb], relation: "app/serializers/rest/account_serializer.rb:29 `Account.model_name` - a SimpleDelegator wrapper asks the class for its ActiveModel name so a moved-account delegate serialises as an account"}
  - {id: d:fetch-remote-actor-typecheck, group: dependents, match: [fetch_remote_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/activitypub/fetch_remote_account_service.rb:7 `return actor if actor.nil? || actor.is_a?(Account)` - remote actor fetching type-tests the fetched actor against the class and rejects anything else"}
  - {id: d:admin-deletion-worker, group: dependents, match: [admin/account_deletion_worker.rb], relation: "app/workers/admin/account_deletion_worker.rb:9 `delete_account = Account.find(account_id)` - the deletion job rehydrates the record from an id carried across the queue boundary"}
  - {id: d:self-destruct-inboxes, group: dependents, match: [scheduler/self_destruct_scheduler.rb], relation: "app/workers/scheduler/self_destruct_scheduler.rb:54 `@inboxes ||= Account.inboxes` - the instance self-destruct scheduler memoises every known delivery inbox off the class before tearing the instance down"}
  - {id: d:account-batch-form, group: dependents, match: [form/account_batch.rb], relation: "app/models/form/account_batch.rb:69 `Account.where(id: account_ids)` - the admin bulk-action form resolves its checked ids back to records through the class"}
  - {id: d:action-log-filter, group: dependents, match: [admin/action_log_filter.rb], relation: "app/models/admin/action_log_filter.rb:114 `account = Account.find_or_initialize_by(id: value)` - filtering the audit log by target builds an unsaved account to match the polymorphic target on"}
  - {id: d:note-length-mention-re, group: dependents, match: [validators/note_length_validator.rb], relation: "app/validators/note_length_validator.rb:19 `new_text.gsub!(Account::MENTION_RE, '@\\2')` - the profile-note length validator strips mentions using a pattern constant declared on the class before counting graphemes"}
  - {id: d:extractor-mention-re, group: dependents, match: [lib/extractor.rb], relation: "app/lib/extractor.rb:28 `text.scan(Account::MENTION_RE) do |screen_name, _|` - the text-entity extractor drives its mention scan off a pattern constant declared on the class"}
  - {id: d:space-usage-avatar-sum, group: dependents, match: [dimension/space_usage_dimension.rb], relation: "app/lib/admin/metrics/dimension/space_usage_dimension.rb:44 `Account.sum(Arel.sql('COALESCE(avatar_file_size, 0) + COALESCE(header_file_size, 0)'))` - the admin storage dashboard aggregates avatar and header bytes over the class"}
  - {id: d:statuses-index-local-votes, group: dependents, match: [importer/statuses_index_importer.rb], relation: "app/lib/importer/statuses_index_importer.rb:74 `Poll.joins(:votes).where(votes: { account: Account.local })` - the search-index importer restricts which poll votes get indexed by joining against the class's local scope"}
  - {id: d:accounts-index-searchable, group: dependents, match: [importer/accounts_index_importer.rb], relation: "app/lib/importer/accounts_index_importer.rb:28 `Account.searchable` - the account search-index importer takes the whole set it indexes from one named class scope"}
